It contains short verbatim excerpts (definitions and theorem statements) from the
following projects, reproduced to allow independent cross-checking of statements:

1. google-deepmind/formal-conjectures (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures),
   commit 2a315101d438a68c0ebdf22b7485ac731624efed.
   Copyright The Formal Conjectures Authors. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
   Excerpts:
   - FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Coloring/List.lean
     (`IsKChoosable`, `listChromaticNumber`), in
     RecentResults/NoelListTotalColouring.lean and RecentResults/NoelPartialListColouring.lean.
   - FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/33.lean (`AdditiveBasisCondition` and the statement of
     `erdos_33.variants.vanDoorn`), in RecentResults/Erdos33.lean.
   Modifications: namespaces changed; attributes removed.

2. facebookresearch/atlas-lean (https://github.com/facebookresearch/atlas-lean),
   commit 8920030e01896f93ad131e164cc11f98c91a44d8.
   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0, with the notice reproduced in
   third_party/atlas-lean-LICENSE ("Codebase is not intended for use to train, fine-tune,
   distill, evaluate, or otherwise develop ML models").
   Excerpts:
   - MathlibExt/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/ListColoring.lean (`IsListColorable`,
     `IsChoiceNumber`);
   - WantedExt/Combinatorics/PartialListColoringProportionalBoundWanted.lean and
     WantedExt/GraphTheory/PartialListColoringWanted.lean (definitions and the
     `conjecture` statements);
   all in RecentResults/NoelPartialListColouring.lean.
   Modifications: namespaces changed; some docstrings shortened; proofs of the
   `theorem_wanted` targets added.

3. plby/lean-proofs (https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs), which carries no license.
   Only the four-line mathematical statement of
   `BasisSubset.basis_sumset_cardinality_bound` (|A| + |B| ≥ 2n) is restated, in
   RecentResults/XuAdditiveBases.lean, to show that it follows from our Theorem 1.1.
   No proof code from that repository is used.

The files in Fidelity/Blind/ quote single theorem statements from the cited papers, with
attribution, for the purpose of checking formalization fidelity.
